    {
     "p": "Let $A$ denote a later outreach need and $B$ a positive screening flag. $P(A\\cap B)$ is the probability both occur; $P(A\\mid B)=P(A\\cap B)/P(B)$ if $P(B)>0$. The product identity $P(A\\cap B)=P(B\\mid A)P(A)$ produces Bayes' rule $P(A\\mid B)=P(B\\mid A)P(A)/P(B)$. The denominator can be expanded by a partition: $P(B)=P(B\\mid A)P(A)+P(B\\mid\\neg A)P(\\neg A)$."
    },
    {
     "p": "Suppose 2% of visits need outreach, screening catches 90% of those visits, and 10% of visits that do not need outreach also flag. For 10,000 visits, expected true positives are $10{,}000(0.02)(0.9)=180$ and expected false positives are $10{,}000(0.98)(0.1)=980$. Among positive flags, the expected share truly needing outreach is $180/(180+980)\\approx0.1552$. A 90% sensitivity is not a 90% positive predictive value. The latter depends strongly on prevalence and specificity in the population being screened."
    },
    {
     "p": "Independence is $P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)$; mutually exclusive nonempty events have $P(A\\cap B)=0$ and are generally not independent. Conditional independence means $P(A,B\\mid C)=P(A\\mid C)P(B\\mid C)$ for the conditioning values where defined. A naive Bayes classifier assumes feature independence conditional on the class, an approximation useful even when it is not literally true; correlated features can double-count evidence and harm probability calibration. Check model behavior on held-out data rather than calling the conditional-independence assumption a proved fact."
    },
    {
     "p": "**Worked check:** If the screen's false-positive rate falls from 10% to 1% with the same prevalence and sensitivity, expected false positives become 98, and the positive predictive value becomes $180/(180+98)\\approx0.6475$. The same positive result now means something different because the test specificity changed."
    }
