   "heading": "2.8.4 A tiny calculation and implementation checks",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "The code verifies the one-dimensional support points, hinge contributions, and an RBF Gram matrix. It does not train an SVM or measure field accuracy."
    },
    {
     "code": "import numpy as np\n\nx=np.array([0.,1.,3.,4.])\ny=np.array([-1.,-1.,1.,1.])\nw=1.; b=-2.\naligned=y*(w*x+b)\nhinge=np.maximum(0,1-aligned)\nassert np.all(aligned>=1)\nassert np.allclose(aligned,[2.,1.,1.,2.])\nassert np.allclose(hinge,0)\nassert np.isclose(1/abs(w),1.)\nassert np.isclose(2/abs(w),2.)\nassert np.isclose(max(0,1-1*.2),.8)\nassert np.isclose(max(0,1-(-1)*.2),1.2)\n\ngamma=.5\ngram=np.exp(-gamma*(x[:,None]-x[None,:])**2)\nassert np.allclose(gram,gram.T)\nassert np.linalg.eigvalsh(gram).min()>-1e-10"
    },
    {
     "p": "The functional margin $y_i(wx_i+b)$ is 1 at $x=1$ and $x=3$, making these the support points in this feasible hard-margin separator. The decision boundary solves $wx+b=0$, hence $x=2$. Each support point is $1/\\lvert w\\rvert=1$ unit from that boundary; the width between the two supporting hyperplanes is 2. Rescaling both $w$ and $b$ multiplies functional margins but leaves the boundary unchanged, which is why the canonical constraint $y_i(wx_i+b)\\geq1$ is needed before interpreting $1/\\lVert w\\rVert$ as geometric margin. Hinge loss is zero on these four points, yet that says nothing about future error. The Gram matrix checks symmetry and positive semidefiniteness of the chosen RBF kernel on this sample. It does not establish that $\\gamma=0.5$ is a good bandwidth; tune bandwidth and regularization using an appropriate group or time split and reserve the final test for one assessment."
    }
   ]
