  {
   "heading": "2.14.1 Voting classification and averaged regression",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "A hard-voting classifier selects the most common class predicted by member models. It discards confidence and needs an explicit tie policy. Soft voting averages predicted class probabilities and selects the largest averaged value; the probabilities need reasonable calibration and comparable class definitions. For weights $w_j\\ge0$, normalized weighted probability for class $c$ is $\\sum_jw_j p_j(c)/\\sum_jw_j$. Choosing weights on the test set contaminates the test. Highly correlated models can agree on the same mistake, so adding members does not guarantee improvement."
    },
    {
     "p": "Suppose three classifiers predict positive-class probabilities $(0.8,0.6,0.1)$ and use threshold 0.5. Hard votes are positive, positive, negative, so majority is positive. Equal-weight soft average is $(0.8+0.6+0.1)/3=0.5$; a convention of 'positive if probability at least 0.5' outputs positive, while a strict greater-than convention would not. Weights $(1,1,3)$ give $(0.8+0.6+3(0.1))/5=0.34$, predicting negative at 0.5. Neither outcome is intrinsically correct; justify weights and threshold using development data and real intervention costs."
    },
    {
     "p": "For regression, averaged predictions $\\hat y=\\sum_jw_j\\hat y_j/\\sum_jw_j$ can reduce error when member errors complement one another. If two unbiased predictors have independent errors with variance $\\sigma^2$, their equal average has error variance $\\sigma^2/2$. If their errors have correlation $\\rho$, variance is $\\sigma^2(1+\\rho)/2$; when $\\rho=1$, averaging provides no variance reduction. Biases also remain in the average. Models trained on different data must use a consistent target definition and the same prediction horizon."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "2.14.2 Bagging, random feature selection, and variance",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "Bootstrap aggregation draws training datasets by sampling rows with replacement, fits a model on each sample, and averages or votes. A bootstrap sample of size $n$ includes about $n(1-(1-1/n)^n)$ distinct original rows in expectation; for large $n$, the fraction approaches $1-e^{-1}\\approx0.632$. An observation omitted from one tree's bootstrap sample is out-of-bag for that tree and can be predicted by those trees. Out-of-bag estimates can be useful internal diagnostics under suitable exchangeable sampling, but a temporal deployment claim still needs a future-time evaluation, and grouped vehicles must not be treated as independent rows."
    },
    {
     "p": "Random forests add randomized feature consideration at tree splits, reducing similarity among trees when a few strong predictors would otherwise dominate every split. A decision tree can fit a high-variance rule on one training sample; averaging less-correlated trees often stabilizes predictions. The relation is qualitative because trees can be biased, correlated, and affected by distribution shift. Feature subsampling is not automatically beneficial for every dataset; tune it within validation folds and inspect performance at fixed resource limits."
    },
    {
     "p": "For event-count regression, a forest prediction is an average of leaf predictions. Its standard averaging of observed training outcomes does not generally extrapolate beyond the range of outcomes represented in relevant leaves, a concern when future bus usage exceeds historical levels. Split on vehicle groups and later months to test whether a forest recognizes patterns that travel across vehicles rather than memorizing vehicle IDs or maintenance practices."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "2.14.3 Additive boosting and second-order tree fitting",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "Gradient boosting constructs an additive predictor $F_M(x)=F_0(x)+\\sum_{m=1}^M\\eta f_m(x)$, fitting each new weak learner to improve an objective relative to the existing ensemble. For squared error $\\ell(y,F)=(y-F)^2/2$, negative derivative with respect to $F$ is $y-F$, so a next tree can fit residual-like targets. For binary logistic loss with logit $F$, the negative derivative is $y-\\sigma(F)$. A fitted tree can use leaf-specific updates and regularization; 'fit the residual' is an intuition, not the complete formula for every loss."
    },
    {
     "p": "For a second-order expansion of per-row loss around current score $F_i$, let $g_i=\\partial\\ell_i/\\partial F_i$ and $h_i=\\partial^2\\ell_i/\\partial F_i^2$. A proposed leaf with constant output $w$ has approximate objective $G w+(H+\\lambda)w^2/2$, where $G=\\sum_{i\\in leaf}g_i$, $H=\\sum h_i$, and $\\lambda\\ge0$ penalizes leaf weight. Derivative is $G+(H+\\lambda)w$, giving optimum $w^*=-G/(H+\\lambda)$ when denominator positive. Its objective improvement against a zero-weight leaf is $G^2/[2(H+\\lambda)]$ before split and tree-complexity penalties. A proposed split compares the left and right leaf improvements with the unsplit parent and subtracts any split penalty."
    },
    {
     "p": "Numerical check: for a leaf with $G=-6,H=4,\\lambda=2$, weight is $6/6=1$ and approximate improvement is $36/(2\\cdot6)=3$. If splitting creates left $(G_L,H_L)=(-4,2)$ and right $(G_R,H_R)=(-2,2)$, leaf improvements are $16/[2(2+2)]=2$ and $4/[2(2+2)]=0.5$, total 2.5, below unsplit improvement 3. Without other considerations, the split is not worthwhile. These numbers explain an objective calculation; implementation details such as histogram thresholds, missing-value routing, and categorical handling are algorithm-specific and need their own tested setup."
    },
    {
     "p": "Boosting can overfit by adding too many trees or too much leaf complexity. A learning rate, depth, leaf size, regularization, and early stopping can limit it. Early stopping needs an appropriate validation period distinct from the final test. If rare events shift in prevalence, a well-ranked boosted score can still be poorly calibrated; inspect probabilities and operating thresholds separately."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "2.14.4 Ordered categorical statistics and leakage in boosted trees",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "A target-encoded category uses labeled outcomes to estimate how that category relates to the target. On a training row with a rare category seen only once, using the category's full-data average reveals the row's own label. A model can memorize this apparent perfect predictor. Out-of-fold training encodings avoid this self-inclusion by producing each row's encoding from other folds. An ordered scheme uses earlier rows in a randomized training permutation to construct a category statistic for a later row, with a prior to handle unseen categories; the precise algorithm can include additional steps beyond this principle."
    },
    {
     "p": "As a simple sequence, consider category `route A` with past binary labels $(1,0,1)$. Before the first label, with prior rate 0.4 and prior weight 2, estimate $(0+2(0.4))/(0+2)=0.4$. Before the second, estimate $(1+0.8)/(1+2)=0.6$. Before the third, estimate $(1+0+0.8)/(2+2)=0.45$. The third row's own label 1 cannot enter its own encoding. For validation or test rows, calculate the mapping from eligible training rows only. If the ordered sequence is genuinely temporal, respect actual cutoff times as well as any randomized training scheme; a training permutation cannot legalize information from the future."
    },
    {
     "p": "This chapter explains leakage-safe categorical evidence and tree boosting. It does not assume that every boosting library uses identical formulas or defaults. Evaluate categorical cardinality, unseen routes, vehicle-level splitting, and the cost of storing category statistics before treating a tool's apparent training gain as generalization."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "2.14.5 Stacking, blending, and model explanations",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "Stacking fits a second-level model on predictions from base learners. To prevent target leakage, build training inputs for the second-level learner from **out-of-fold base predictions**: for each fold, fit each base model on the other folds and predict the held-out fold. Concatenate these predictions with labels to train the meta-model. Then refit base models on all eligible development data and use their predictions as meta-model inputs for a future test set. If the meta-model is trained on base predictions produced by base models that already saw the same labels, it can learn to exploit overfitting rather than genuine complementary information. Blending reserves a development holdout for meta-model fitting, reducing available base-model training data but simplifying the workflow."
    },
    {
     "p": "Feature importance asks different questions depending on its definition. Impurity-based importance sums within-tree split reductions and can favor variables offering many candidate cuts. Permutation importance measures performance change after disrupting a feature on a specified held-out distribution; it can understate importance when correlated features substitute for one another and can create unrealistic feature combinations. Local explanations approximate why a fitted model scored a particular row under a chosen method; they do not establish causal consequences of changing that row's feature. For an actionable bus-maintenance explanation, trace input timestamps, data-quality warnings, and actual intervention evidence along with any importance score."
    },
    {
     "p": "**Worked capacity decision:** Two boosted models have similar ROC AUC, but one selects 40 buses for inspection and finds 30 true cases, while the other selects 80 and finds 34. Their precisions are $30/40=0.75$ and $34/80=0.425$. If the team can inspect 40 buses, compare both models' top-40 yield on future vehicles; the top-80 report does not measure the same feasible policy. Tune capacity using development data and report untouched test results afterward."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "2.14.6 Test a forest's feature reliance on held-out units",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "An importance score needs a definition before it can be interpreted. For a classification split with a positive proportion $p$, Gini impurity is $G(p)=1-p^2-(1-p)^2=2p(1-p)$. A split that moves rows from a parent into children with proportions $p_L,p_R$ reduces node impurity by $G(p)-\\frac{n_L}{n}G(p_L)-\\frac{n_R}{n}G(p_R)$. A forest's impurity-based feature score aggregates such decreases over trees, usually weighted by node sample counts, and then normalizes across features. It answers which variables this fitted forest used during training. It can favor continuous or high-cardinality features because they offer many possible split points, and it does not by itself estimate what happens on new sites."
    },
    {
     "p": "Permutation importance answers a different empirical question. Choose a metric $S$ on an untouched evaluation set, fix the fitted model $f$, and compute $I_j=S(f,X,y)-\\mathbb E_{\\pi}[S(f,X_{\\pi(j)},y)]$. Here $\\pi(j)$ permutes just feature $j$ across the evaluation rows. A positive value means disrupting that feature harms the measured score on that evaluation population. It is **not a causal effect**: permutation can create implausible feature combinations and correlated substitutes can hide one another's importance. The metric is part of the definition. A feature may matter for top-five recall but hardly change accuracy if positives are rare. Report the permutation distribution, base score, held-out population, and sampling unit alongside the importance."
    },
    {
     "p": "**Executable comparison on invented measurements**"
    },
    {
     "p": "The independent dataset contains one invented record per refrigeration unit. Unit-level training and test rows do not overlap; the first sensor is strongly informative by construction and the second is weaker. The calculation checks that the two importance methods are defined and that the test performance metric is measured outside training. Its asserted properties are deliberately modest because scores depend on a particular synthetic sample."
    },
    {
     "code": "import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nfrom sklearn.inspection import permutation_importance\nfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\n\nrng=np.random.default_rng(377)\nsensor=rng.normal(size=440)\ncycles=rng.normal(size=440)\nunrelated=rng.normal(size=440)\nX=np.column_stack((sensor,cycles,unrelated))\ny=(sensor+0.35*cycles+0.2*rng.normal(size=440)>0).astype(int)\nX_train,X_test=X[:320],X[320:]\ny_train,y_test=y[:320],y[320:]\nmodel=RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=80,min_samples_leaf=4,\n                             max_features=2,random_state=377,n_jobs=1)\nmodel.fit(X_train,y_train)\nbase=roc_auc_score(y_test,model.predict_proba(X_test)[:,1])\npermuted=permutation_importance(model,X_test,y_test,scoring='roc_auc',\n                                n_repeats=12,random_state=377,n_jobs=1)\nassert 0.5<=base<=1\nassert len(model.feature_importances_)==X.shape[1]\nassert np.isclose(model.feature_importances_.sum(),1)\nassert permuted.importances.shape==(X.shape[1],12)\nassert permuted.importances_mean[0]>0"
    },
    {
     "p": "The positive last assertion is a check of this particular invented dataset, not a universal property of forest importance. A negative permutation score can arise from sampling noise or a genuinely harmful feature. Reusing these test results to pick tree depth or engineer new features consumes the holdout; reserve a later test or carry out exploration on validation folds. If depot or unit measurements repeat, split and permute at the appropriate group level. If `cycles` and `sensor` are nearly duplicates, permuting either one alone can underestimate their combined usefulness; inspect grouped feature removal or retrain ablations on a fresh validation design. To make an explanation actionable, connect a high-impact feature to a valid data source, model error slices, and a feasible decision. **Exercises:** (1) Replace AUC with precision at a fixed inspection capacity; explain why the ordering of importance can change. (2) Duplicate the first feature and compare the two individual permutation results. (3) Describe a time-aware evaluation in which an alert field created after dispatch is excluded even if it appears important in a historical table."
    },
    {
     "figure": {
      "src": "/theory/figures/textbook/29_forest_importance.svg",
      "alt": "Training impurity and held-out AUC permutation importance on invented units",
      "caption": "**Figure — two importance definitions.** The left bars are normalized training split-impurity shares and the right bars are average held-out AUC drops with variation over independent permutations. Bar heights have different units and must not be compared numerically across panels; the plot shows how two definitions answer different questions on the same invented dataset."
     }
    },
    {
     "p": "**Related reading:** Cross-validation design establishes the population for importance; decision-tree split criteria derive the impurity reductions being aggregated."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "2.5.4 Voting, stacking, and the origin of meta-features",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "Hard voting combines base-model class labels. Soft voting averages estimated class probabilities, possibly with weights. If component probabilities for a pack are (0.10,0.30,0.80), an equal average is 0.40. This average can be miscalibrated if the components are miscalibrated, have different base-rate assumptions, or make strongly correlated errors. Choose weights on development data and report the operational policy alongside them."
    },
    {
     "p": "Stacking trains a meta-model on base-model predictions. To build meta-training data without obvious self-fitting leakage, divide development records into appropriate folds. For each fold, fit each base model on other folds only and predict rows in the held-out fold. Every development row then receives out-of-fold base predictions. Train the meta-model on those predictions and their labels. For later inference, refit base models on the allowed full development data and feed their predictions through the trained combiner. The meta-model must not see a score produced by a base model trained on that same row. With correlated repeated packs, folds need to keep whole pack IDs together, and with forecasting they must respect time."
    },
    {
     "p": "For three small development folds F1,F2,F3, a row in F2 receives scores from models trained on F1+F3, never from a model fitted on F2. A trained combiner can exploit complementary strengths, but it can also learn that a base model memorized a site or training label if the fold design is wrong. A dedicated validation subset used to train the combiner is often called blending; it leaves fewer rows for base-model fitting but has a simpler origin trail. Compare any complex stack against a simple average under identical future evaluation."
    },
    {
     "p": "The following code constructs an **index plan** for out-of-fold fitting. It does not fit models and therefore cannot claim a stack was trained; it verifies the critical non-overlap of fit and prediction indices before implementation."
    },
    {
     "code": "def out_of_fold_index_plan(groups):\n    unique = list(dict.fromkeys(groups))\n    if len(unique) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Need at least two groups\")\n    plan = []\n    for holdout in unique:\n        fit = [i for i, group in enumerate(groups) if group != holdout]\n        predict = [i for i, group in enumerate(groups) if group == holdout]\n        assert set(fit).isdisjoint(predict)\n        assert {groups[i] for i in fit}.isdisjoint({groups[i] for i in predict})\n        plan.append((fit, predict))\n    return plan\n\n\nfolds = out_of_fold_index_plan([\"pack-A\", \"pack-A\", \"pack-B\", \"pack-C\"])\nassert sum(len(predict) for _, predict in folds) == 4\nassert any(predict == [0, 1] for _, predict in folds)"
    },
    {
     "p": "This is a leave-one-group-out plan, which can be costly for many unique packs and does not automatically enforce chronology. Design an appropriate group/time plan for the actual deployment task. The principle is the same: every meta-feature should come from a base fit that was not trained on its row or leakage-related group."
    },
    {
     "p": "**Worked exercise.** A base tree scores its own training rows with 99% accuracy and later rows with 70%. A meta-model trained on in-sample tree probabilities learns to rely on unrealistically confident predictions. Generating base probabilities out of fold gives the meta-model a training distribution more like later inference. It does not remove shifts between months; check the final stack on a later untouched period."
    }
   ]
  }
